{:edb/db-before-t 2027 :edb/db-after-t 2028 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535340 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:24:17.406Z" 13194139535340 true] [17592186119848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344743 13194139535340 true] [17592186119848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344750 13194139535340 true] [17592186119849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344757 13194139535340 true] [17592186119849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344764 13194139535340 true] [17592186120078 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535340 true] [17592186120078 1100 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z = x - y$ in $R^{3}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $Π$ is **not** a subspace of $R^{3}$" 13194139535340 false] [17592186120078 1100 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139535340 true] [17592186120078 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}-k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344743 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344744 13194139535340 true] [17592186344743 1089 "q-157374" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344743 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535340 true] [17592186344743 1100 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344743 1113 #uuid "a7f4fe67-ac2a-5712-870b-6d4360b56197" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344743 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344745 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344746 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344747 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344748 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344749 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1041 "selection" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344748 13194139535340 true] [17592186344744 1110 #uuid "abe78eb8-843f-56b1-800d-0f274e699636" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344745 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344745 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344745 1111 #uuid "03dd80ee-f41d-5509-ad9c-c05c03273f0c" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344746 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344746 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344746 1111 #uuid "a89ba1cb-4314-5814-81b4-742ba9dac325" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344747 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344747 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344747 1111 #uuid "160cf484-83ff-5d25-bc60-6834b1410c1e" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344748 1034 "All the statements are true" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344748 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344748 1111 #uuid "ec59aa34-e64b-59d0-81d4-bee9987f0856" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344749 1034 "II only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344749 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344749 1111 #uuid "2262f202-74ef-5cb6-b232-346f8ca18af9" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344750 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344751 13194139535340 true] [17592186344750 1089 "q-157392" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344750 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535340 true] [17592186344750 1100 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,-1,1)$ and $(3,0,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344750 1113 #uuid "b3fe7f00-ceca-5ff9-89b1-e64eba5592d3" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344750 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{2}=y=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-3}{2}\\ne (0)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(4,-1,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(4)-3}{2}\\ne (-1)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344752 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344753 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344754 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344755 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344756 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1041 "selection" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344752 13194139535340 true] [17592186344751 1110 #uuid "5f6ef9ef-3af9-5f5e-ab70-e27b34efe437" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344752 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344752 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344752 1111 #uuid "4b66f96a-6c09-54af-8a04-aea5867e5be2" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344753 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344753 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344753 1111 #uuid "579329cb-477e-5f05-a3da-2a9e3e5e4e11" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344754 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344754 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344754 1111 #uuid "6b8ec876-b437-5013-9fd0-5827aca3894c" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344755 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344755 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344755 1111 #uuid "75d8ad8e-6ffc-595c-b301-e7c2bc72a522" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344756 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344756 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344756 1111 #uuid "0a3a0d17-03d3-5184-8b09-b4c9ce3f7214" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344757 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344758 13194139535340 true] [17592186344757 1089 "q-157469" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344757 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535340 true] [17592186344757 1100 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344757 1113 #uuid "80e9816a-9862-515d-9d15-842549d7a5db" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344757 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+2y-z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z & =1 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+0 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z & =1 \\\\ (1)-2(0)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344759 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344760 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344761 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344762 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344763 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1041 "selection" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344763 13194139535340 true] [17592186344758 1110 #uuid "eb82de6f-56ba-5473-96fa-0bd3564fad41" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344759 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344759 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344759 1111 #uuid "62774d93-60cb-5e5e-a312-4330221063e3" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344760 1034 "III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344760 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344760 1111 #uuid "877386e3-5610-519c-965f-32ca09069de6" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344761 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344761 1111 #uuid "20d202a3-e454-5294-be6e-e2180231e83f" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344762 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344762 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344762 1111 #uuid "52d4a268-4383-57db-821b-160fea5500ab" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344763 1034 "IV only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344763 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344763 1111 #uuid "83a8ea50-12b8-579f-9b84-14fd697c34f9" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344764 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344765 13194139535340 true] [17592186344764 1089 "q-157476" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344764 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535340 true] [17592186344764 1100 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344764 1113 #uuid "7a908341-291b-56eb-a9bd-4b2baf390d35" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344764 1145 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (4)-2(0)+2(1) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344766 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344767 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344768 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344769 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344770 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1041 "selection" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344766 13194139535340 true] [17592186344765 1110 #uuid "a5500e64-f5d8-562b-ae05-1649d58300a9" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344766 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344766 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344766 1111 #uuid "d2e003c8-9e79-51fb-940d-2a4c2fbae91b" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344767 1034 "III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344767 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344767 1111 #uuid "c1e9a86c-f5b6-5acc-96ca-423b5ae8c5ed" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344768 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344768 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344768 1111 #uuid "e8aab954-6ad2-5135-a55f-110b2c7eef7c" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344769 1034 "IV only" 13194139535340 true] [17592186344769 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535340 true] [17592186344769 1111 #uuid "ed1c2756-6144-599a-9eac-cf716c94cb4e" 13194139535340 true] 